What this licence covers, and what it does not

The text above covers this repository's own files: the adapter and the test
runners under waterbox/, the build files, the documents, and the patches under
patches/ (which are changes to Cadmium, itself MIT).

It does not cover what the repository builds from. The emulator is Cadmium, by
Steffen Schuemann (https://github.com/gulrak/cadmium), under the MIT licence,
at the commit pinned in extern/cadmium. With it are compiled: Chiplet, {fmt},
the ghc filesystem and utility headers, nlohmann/json and magic_enum (MIT);
fast_float (MIT, Apache-2.0 or BSL-1.0); bit-lite (BSL-1.0); and a SHA-1
implementation, stdendian and stb_image (public domain). Chiplet's Octo
compiler is a C++ version of the one in John Earnest's C-Octo (MIT).

Cadmium's source contains, as data, the small programs the computers it
emulates were sold or published with: the COSMAC VIP's monitor and RCA's
CHIP-8 interpreters (1977-78), and CHIPOS for the DREAM 6800 (Michael J.
Bauer, 1978; Cadmium's README says it is used with his permission), with
Tobias V. Langhoff's CHIPOSLO. They are in the built core because they are in
Cadmium.

waterbox/package-licenses.json lists every component with its licence and
where it comes from, and the built package carries their licence texts.

No CHIP-8 program is in this repository or in the package. The test programs
the tests run are Cadmium's and C-Octo's own, read from the pinned submodules.
